WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.245
Lesson 10.9.

00:00:02.245 --> 00:00:04.480
Peg and redemption arbitrage.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.839
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:10.409
able to understand the arbitrages that keep stablecoins

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:12.832
and LSTs near their value, and when a

00:00:12.832 --> 00:00:14.745
retail operator can take part.

00:00:16.045 --> 00:00:17.335
Here's the plan.

00:00:17.335 --> 00:00:19.020
First, the key ideas.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.735
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:21.735 --> 00:00:23.260
Then, your checklist.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.422
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:26.722 --> 00:00:32.097
First, Stablecoin peg arbitrage: if a stablecoin trades

00:00:32.097 --> 00:00:34.784
at 0.995 on a DEX and a PSM

00:00:34.784 --> 00:00:38.432
or issuer redeems it at 1.00 (minus a

00:00:38.432 --> 00:00:42.187
fee), buy-and-redeem closes the gap.

00:00:42.187 --> 00:00:45.137
It's competitive, needs fast execution, and is

00:00:45.137 --> 00:00:47.697
often limited to whoever can redeem.

00:00:47.697 --> 00:00:52.321
Next, LST discount arbitrage: if an LST trades below

00:00:52.321 --> 00:00:57.157
its redemption value, buy it and queue a redemption.

00:00:57.157 --> 00:01:00.277
Your return is the discount over the waiting time,

00:01:00.277 --> 00:01:03.213
with the risk that the discount reflects a real

00:01:03.213 --> 00:01:06.817
problem (slashing, a bug) or that the queue lengthens.

00:01:06.817 --> 00:01:10.230
And finally, who wins: professional searchers

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:13.127
take most instant arbitrage (14.6).

00:01:13.127 --> 00:01:15.979
What's left to patient operators is the slow

00:01:15.979 --> 00:01:19.022
kind: buying discounts and waiting, sized small.

00:01:20.322 --> 00:01:22.837
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:22.837 --> 00:01:25.519
An LST trades at a 2% discount;

00:01:25.519 --> 00:01:29.004
the redemption queue is about 20 days.

00:01:29.004 --> 00:01:31.730
Return ≈ 2% over 20 days ≈ 36.5%

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:35.778
annualised (simple), but only this once, only if

00:01:35.778 --> 00:01:39.577
redemption works as expected, and only if the

00:01:39.577 --> 00:01:43.099
discount wasn't pricing a real problem.

00:01:43.099 --> 00:01:46.101
Research why the discount exists first (Module

00:01:46.101 --> 00:01:48.464
6); cap it in the speculative bucket.

00:01:49.764 --> 00:01:52.335
The reason the discount exists: an actual

00:01:52.335 --> 00:01:55.089
problem with the asset, or redemption delays.

00:01:56.389 --> 00:01:57.849
Here's your checklist.

00:01:57.849 --> 00:01:59.384
Do it now, for real.

00:01:59.384 --> 00:02:02.809
First, Redemption path and eligibility confirmed.

00:02:02.809 --> 00:02:05.414
Next, Reason for the discount researched.

00:02:05.414 --> 00:02:07.959
And finally, Queue length and worst case written.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:10.469
Question 1.

00:02:10.469 --> 00:02:12.464
What closes a stablecoin's discount?

00:02:16.884 --> 00:02:22.278
The answer: Arbitrageurs buying below peg and redeeming

00:02:22.278 --> 00:02:25.649
at 1.00 (e.g. via a PSM or issuer).

00:02:26.949 --> 00:02:28.194
Question 2.

00:02:28.194 --> 00:02:31.039
1% discount, 10-day redemption.

00:02:31.039 --> 00:02:32.684
Simple annualised return?

00:02:37.104 --> 00:02:39.889
The answer: About 36.5%.

00:02:41.189 --> 00:02:42.459
Question 3.

00:02:42.459 --> 00:02:43.759
Why might a discount be

00:02:43.759 --> 00:02:45.384
a warning, not an opportunity?

00:02:49.804 --> 00:02:51.783
The answer: It may reflect a

00:02:51.783 --> 00:02:54.854
real problem (slashing, exploit, insolvency).

00:02:56.154 --> 00:02:57.399
Let's recap.

00:02:57.399 --> 00:03:02.185
Stablecoin peg arbitrage: if a stablecoin trades

00:03:02.185 --> 00:03:04.530
at 0.995 on a DEX and a

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:07.753
PSM or issuer redeems it at 1.00

00:03:07.753 --> 00:03:12.449
(minus a fee), buy-and-redeem closes the gap.

00:03:12.449 --> 00:03:16.933
LST discount arbitrage: if an LST trades below its

00:03:16.933 --> 00:03:21.454
redemption value, buy it and queue a redemption.

00:03:21.454 --> 00:03:26.559
Who wins: professional searchers take most instant arbitrage (14.6).

00:03:27.859 --> 00:03:30.374
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:30.374 --> 00:03:33.749
Next up, Lesson 11.0: Mastery Starter.
